---
title: Mushrooms on toast
description: Lesson 1.2. Water has to leave before anything browns.
servings: 2
time: 20 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, heat-in-a-pan]
lesson: "1.2"
technique: browning
status: draft
---

> Technique: water before browning. A mushroom holds a lot of water and
> will not brown until that water has cooked out of it and boiled away.
> So this recipe gets the water out first, on purpose, and browns second.
-- source: Lan Lam, "New-School Sautéed Mushrooms", America's Test Kitchen, 2018-12-05, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/1196-new-school-sauteed-mushrooms

Cut @mushrooms{400%g}(chestnut or white) into quarters.

Put all the mushrooms in a #large frying pan{} with @water{3%tbsp} and set it over high heat. Cook, stirring now and then, for ~{7%minutes}, until the mushrooms have shrunk and fit in one layer, the pan is dry, and the sound has changed from bubbling to sizzling.

> Why: steaming the mushrooms first makes them collapse quickly and
> give up their water. A collapsed mushroom also soaks up far less oil
> than a raw one, which is why the oil goes in only now.

Add @oil{1%tsp} and spread the mushrooms out in one layer. Cook for ~{5%minutes}, stirring only once or twice, until they are well browned.

> Why: you may have been told never to crowd mushrooms. In a 2009
> side-by-side by Dave Arnold, one crowded pan of soaked mushrooms
> looked as good as dry mushrooms cooked in three roomy batches, and
> tasted less oily. It was one informal test, with nothing measured.
-- source: Dave Arnold, "Crowded Wet Mushrooms. A Beautiful Thing", Cooking Issues, 2009-12-21, https://cookingissues.com/2009/12/21/crowded-wet-mushrooms-a-beautiful-thing/

Turn the heat down to low. Add @butter{15%g}, @garlic{1%clove}(finely chopped), @thyme{2%sprigs}(leaves only) and @salt{1/2%tsp}, and stir for ~{1%minute}, until the garlic smells cooked.

Toast @bread{2%thick slices}. Pile the mushrooms on the toast and finish with a squeeze of @lemon{1/2}.

> Next time, without the recipe: any mushrooms, all in the pan at once
> with a splash of water. Cook until the pan is dry and sizzling, add a
> little fat, then leave them alone until brown. Season at the end.
